a campaign to win hearts and minds  senegal's ruling party coalition  candidate alpha c  wants to become the mayor of ghingenio  once a thriving railway town abandoned  by the state  gone are there generations of syrian  lebanese and malians  who settled by the railway line  exporting textiles peanuts and cotton  this no longer the gateway to africa it  is now a place where the young want to  leave to go to europe  alpha c wants to see ginginio return to  its former glory  senegal mostly coming out of the big  cities is a very poor country these  people  are in need of new infrastructure  they're in need of a new hope even  something that they can rely on there's  nothing worse than use that don't have a  hope of a better future at the  inauguration of a new intercity rail  line in dakar president makisel promises  a better future despite an economic boom  many young people feel left behind  in march nationwide protests erupted  triggered by the arrest of opposition  leader usmansanko people looted foreign  supermarkets businesses denouncing  growing inequality poverty and  unemployment  the populist politician sanko is running  for mayor in the southern town of  zigesha  there is no way that my political party  will lose these local elections  we have come here to strengthen our  political foundation  this is where we originate from at the  last presidential elections i won most  of the votes here i have come to confirm  my support and safeguarding  but for voters many of the pledges made  by past mayors remain unfulfilled in  ginginio the promised football stadium  is health built and the ice factory is  still not completed  people are still waiting for the  promised development funds coming from  dakar to arrive to this remote railway  town  but in certain areas there's still no  electricity or running water and there  hasn't been a train running by for  almost two decades there's a sense of  frustration  among people here towards the political  system  and politicians and the challenge for  candidates is to try to convince people  to come out and vote in this local  election  with over 225 thousand candidates most  of them running for the first time  this poll could become a turning point  changing senegal's political landscape  nicholas hawk al-jazeera gingenu
 

